TI  - REPELLENT TO PROTECT RADIATA PINE-SEEDLINGS FROM BROWSING BY SHEEP
T2  - NEW ZEALAND JOURNAL OF FORESTRY SCIENCE
AB  - Repellents containing either thiram or egg, together with acrylic adhesive and red dye, were sprayed on 1-yr-old radiata pine seedlings. Browsing damage on the seedlings by sheep was compared, together with that on untreated seedlings, over a 6-mo. period. Formulations containing egg significantly reduced the incidence of browsing for 3-4 mo., but mixtures containing thiram, or acrylic adhesive and red dye without egg were not effective repellents. Where radiata pine is planted on pasture and sheep are grazed among the seedlings, egg-based repellents may be useful in reducing spring and early summer browsing damage. They could also be used on forest sites to protect seedlings from sheep introduced to control weeds such as bracken fern.
