TI  - RESTORATION OF SWAMPY LAND MASSES BY THE USE OF A PRIMITIVE BOVINE RACE (HIGHLAND CATTLE) - CASE OF THE NATURE-RESERVE-OF-MANNEVILLES (VERNIER-BOG, EURE)
T2  - BULLETIN D ECOLOGIE
AB  - With the evolution of agronomy, secondrate lands such as marshes were abandoned. In the Vernier Bog (Eure, France) the stopping of pasturing and the lack of herbivorous herds led to rapid modification of the ecosystems, characterized, at first, by the development of a highly productive vegetation composed of low herbaceous plants, biologically diversified and typical of marshes. These ecosystems have developed into dry woodlands which most of the animals and plants specific to humid areas have deserted. An attempt to create extensive grazing with the introduction of Highland Cattle, known for being primitive and for possessing physiological and morphological capacities to adapt to such an environment, has shown that a diversified and productive ecosystem can be restored. The interrelations between cattle grazing, microbial activity of the soil, the flora and the wildlife show that it is necessary to perform such an experiment within the natural reserve.
