AU  - FUENTES, ER
AU  - AVILES, R
AU  - SEGURA, A
TI  - THE NATURAL VEGETATION OF A HEAVILY MAN-TRANSFORMED LANDSCAPE - THE SAVANNA OF CENTRAL CHILE
T2  - INTERCIENCIA
AB  - The Intermediate Depression of central Chile is one of the most heavily transformed landscapes in that country. Nowadays it is covered by an Acacia caven savanna with some Prosopis chilensis trees. It is still a matter of debate whether this is the natural vegetation or a man-made formation, a by product of the degradation of former sclerophyllous forests. The current absence of spontaneous colonization by sclerophyllous species cannot be interpreted as evidence supporting the absence of sclerophyllous elements in the natural vegetation of the area because current sources of propagules are too distant and there are now introduced herbivores capable of killing all seedlings. This situation, as other similar ones where the vegetation has been heavily transformed by human use, need additional criteria for their clarification. Here, we propose an experimental criterium that could be of more general use. We tested the hypothesis that, in the absence of the above described constraints, seedlings of Prosopis chilensis and Acacia caven survive better than comparable seedlings of two of the most common evergreen shrubs in the general area (Lithrea caustica and Quillaja saponaria), a requirement of by the hypothesis that the natural vegetation of the area is a savanna dominated by Prospois and Acacia. Results of an experiment with exclosures showed that, after the most critical first dry season, seedlings of the four species exhibit similar survivals. These  findings are consistent with the hypothesis that previously to the arrival of Spanish conquistadors the vegetation of the Intermediate Depression could have been a mixed sclerophyllous species community in which at least Prosopis, Acacia, Lithrea and Quillaja were present. From a biographical perspective our results suggest that in Chile a mediterranean-type climate favours a vegetation with sclerophyllous elements, just as elsewhere, and not a savanna dominated by winter deciduous trees as claimed by supporters of the Prosopis-Acacia hypothesis.
