TY  - CPAPER
AU  - Laspidou, CS
ED  - Lekkas, TD
TI  - Constructed wetlands technology and water quality improvement: Recent advances
T2  - Proceeding of the 9th International Conference on Environmental Science and Technology Vol B - Poster Presentations
CP  - 9th International Conference on Environmental Science and Technology
AB  - Today's demands for improved water quality in receiving waters are widespread and require the implementation of systems that are natural, low-cost and minimal-maintenance that could effectively treat polluted discharges. Wetlands are such systems and are recently receiving a lot of attention from scientists, ecologists and engineers, as they are deemed appropriate for reducing the impact of effluent and run-off on receiving waters. Since a large part of natural wetlands have been lost-about 53% of them in the United States from the 1780s to the 1980s-management options for improving receiving water quality, water reclamation and reuse involve the application of constructed wetlands technology.
   Current applications of constructed wetlands technology include the treatment of primary settled and secondary treated sewage, tertiary effluent polishing, disinfection, urban and rural run-off management, toxicant management, land-fill and mining leachate treatment, sludge management, industrial effluent treatment, enhancement of instream nutrient assimilation, nutrient removal via biomass production and export, and groundwater recharge. Treatment systems include subsurface flow and vertical flow wetlands, passive treatment systems, and simple flow-through systems. The literature is reviewed for a number of different applications of constructed wetlands that result in water quality improvement, for a variety of environmental settings and geographic locations. Current constructed wetlands technology practices are presented.
   Major constructed wetlands research work needs to be directed at refining our understanding on all physical-chemical and biological processes that take place in a wetland to result in nutrient immobilization and other pollutant processing mechanisms. A "user-friendly" methodology of design criteria and planning processes needs to be established to further promote the construction of wetlands, in order to reap their unique benefits in water quality improvement in a natural way. Mathematical modeling of those processes can help that way, as well as the development and refinement of information databases and decision support systems, so as to employ the most environmentally and economically advantageous systems for a given application and locality. The successful creation and restoration of wetlands is based on the concept of self-design, whereby the ecosystem adapts and changes according to its physical constraints.
