ED  - Nielsen, J
ED  - Dodson, JJ
ED  - Friedland, K
ED  - Hamon, TR
ED  - Musick, J
ED  - Verspoor, E
TI  - Conservation of fish and fisheries in large river systems
T2  - RECONCILING FISHERIES WITH CONSERVATION, VOLS I AND II
CP  - 4th World Fisheries Congress
AB  - Large rivers and their associated seasonal wetlands are among the most threatened environments on the planet. On one hand, fishing pressure is severe, but this alone does not produce the large-scale changes in fish populations and fish production that is being witnessed worldwide. On the other hand, engineering for energy, transport, and water abstractions for agriculture are modifying the physical nature and functioning of many rivers. This involves the increasing separation of the river channel from its floodplain, suppression of floodplain waterbodies, and the systematic straightening and regulation of the main river channel. In many rivers, a combination of bad fishing practices and environmental degradation is leading to the disappearance of larger native fish species and their replacement by smaller native and introduced species. As a result, fishery catches are falling. The role of overfishing, pollution, and dams in such changes is well established. Less well understood are the impacts of changes in flow regimes in donor and recipient rivers caused by water abstractions and transfers. This growing problem arises mainly from the needs of irrigated agriculture. Changes in flow regimes produce alterations in fish stocks that are strongly adapted to the regular seasonal recurrence of annual floods. They result in failure to inundate the floodplain, inadequate water in the system at critical times of year to support fish, and shifting of flood peaks to times inappropriate to the natural breeding cycle of the fish. Strategies to rectify these trends need to address, first, the fishery through improved management and, second, the environment, to ensure that the conditions necessary for the sustainability of native fish communities are present. This may best be achieved though preservation of systems in which natural flood patterns and river systems still exist; rehabilitation of damaged systems to restore portions of the floodplain and floodplain waterbodies; and establishment and enforcement of environmental flows that correspond to the needs of the fish populations and fisheries. Each of these approaches poses political, economic, social, and technological problems which, if not solved, will result in the decline of fish biodiversity and fisheries from many of the world's rivers.
