TI  - Applying science to conservation and restoration of the world's wetlands
T2  - WATER SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY
CP  - 14th Stockholm Water Symposium
AB  - The world has an estimated 7 to 9 million km(2) of wetlands which can be defined through their hydrology, physiochemical environment, and biota. Many human cultures have lived in harmony with wetland environments for centuries. Many others have not, resulting in drainage or severe impact of wetlands throughout the world. Conservation of wetlands needs to be a priority for the cultural and ecological values 2 they provide. But a more optimistic note is that large-scale restoration and re-creation of wetlands and riverine systems is beginning to happen throughout the world through ecological engineering. Examples of co large-scale wetland restoration projects are presented for Delaware Bay, the Skjern River (Denmark), Florida V Everglades, Louisiana Delta, the Mississippi River Basin, and the Mesopotamian Marshlands of Iraq.
